.TH MDNSD.CONF 5 "October 2026" "mdnsd 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
mdnsd.conf \- configuration file of the mdnsd daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mdnsd (8)
reads its configuration from
.IR /etc/mdnsd.conf ,
or from the file named on its command line.
The file is read once, at startup.
.PP
The syntax is systemd-style INI:
.IP \(bu 2
Settings are
.I Key=Value
lines in
.I [Section]
blocks.
Section and key names are case-insensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments; blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
A value may be wrapped in single or double quotes:
.BR "Name=\(dqIoT to br0\(dq" .
.IP \(bu 2
Repeating a key appends to a list value such as
.BR Interfaces= ;
an empty assignment resets the list.
.IP \(bu 2
Booleans accept
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false ,
.BR on / off
and
.BR 1 / 0 .
.IP \(bu 2
A line ending in a backslash continues on the next line, as in systemd unit
files.
.PP
Syntax errors and unknown settings are reported with the line they are on.
Unknown sections and keys are errors, so that typos fail loudly.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [Ipc]
Optional.
.TP
.B Socket=
Path of the control socket used by
.BR mdnsctl (1).
Default
.IR /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock .
.IP
The directory must exist and be writable by the daemon; the systemd service
creates
.I /run/mdnsd
for it.
Unix socket paths are limited to about 108 bytes.
A stale socket left by a crashed run is replaced; a live instance, or a file
that is not a socket, is never overwritten.
.TP
.B Mode=
Who may use the control socket, as an octal mode, as systemd writes them:
.IR 0666 ,
the default, lets every user run
.BR mdnsctl ;
.I 0660
limits it to the daemon's user and group (the service's
.IR mdnsd ),
and
.I 0600
to the daemon's user.
What the socket tells is what devices multicast on the link, which any user can
listen for on port 5353, and the one request that does anything,
.BR probe ,
makes the daemon ask its devices again: one asked for while another runs is
that one, so clients cannot make it send more than the probe's few questions
every few seconds.
A directory the socket is in must let its users through: the service's
.I /run/mdnsd
is created with mode 0755.
.TP
.B AvahiSocket=
A second socket, with the protocol of avahi-daemon's, for what asks Avahi for
the address of a name:
.B nss-mdns
(a build of it that has no other way to look a name up is the one Arch ships),
which makes
.B ping pc.local
work with Avahi gone.
Set it to
.IR /run/avahi-daemon/socket ,
where that looks.
Off unless set.
.IP
It answers
.BR RESOLVE-HOSTNAME-IPV4 ,
.B RESOLVE-HOSTNAME-IPV6
and
.B RESOLVE-ADDRESS
of what is known of every segment, and of the names this host advertises, at
the addresses it has: one address, a routable one before a link-local one.
A name not known is asked for on every interface, and waited for up to two
seconds, or a third of one if the name is known at an address of another
family.
Only names under
.B .local
are answered for.
The socket is made for anyone to connect to, as Avahi's is, and tells only what
any host on the links hears.
.IP
The directory must exist and be writable by the daemon, and Avahi must not be
running, or it has the socket.
With the service of the package, a drop-in makes the directory:
.IP
.nf
# systemctl edit mdnsd
[Service]
RuntimeDirectory=avahi-daemon
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0755
.fi
.SS [Advertise]
Optional, and there may be any number of them: each is a name of this host,
with its own services, interfaces and TTL.
Each has a
.BR Hostname= ;
one name is not advertised twice on the same interface.
.TP
.B Hostname=
A name under
.B .local
that the daemon announces, and answers queries for, with this host's own
addresses on each interface it listens on, for example
.BR router.local .
A bare label such as
.B router
means
.BR router.local .
.IP
Each interface is told all of the addresses this host has on it, of the
families listened to, link-local ones too: an A record for each IPv4 address
and an AAAA record for each IPv6 one.
An interface that has no address of a family is skipped, with a message in
the log.
The addresses are read again whenever they change.
.IP
The name is announced and kept alive like a reflected device, answers queries
(a reflected device without services), and is withdrawn with a goodbye on
shutdown.
No conflict detection is done: the name is assumed to be yours alone.
.TP
.B Interface=
The interfaces to announce the name and its services on, and to answer for
there, separated by spaces or commas; the setting may be repeated, and an
empty one clears the list.
Each must be in a segment's
.BR Interfaces= ,
or matched by a pattern in it, and may be a pattern.
Default: all of them.
Needs
.BR Hostname= .
.TP
.B Services=
Services this host offers, announced under the name and answered for, as a
type and a port:
.BR _ssh._tcp:22 .
They are separated by spaces or commas, and the setting may be repeated; an
empty one clears the list.
Each service is of an instance named for the host, so with
.B Hostname=pc.local
it is
.BR pc._ssh._tcp.local ,
as Avahi names one
.B %h
in a service file.
The daemon does not look whether anything is listening there, and has no text
record to give them.
Needs
.BR Hostname= .
.TP
.B TTL=
How long other devices keep the address of the name, as a time span such as
.B 2min
(the default, as Avahi used for host names).
On an interface of a segment with
.BR Reannounce=yes ,
it is announced again at 80% of this, so by default every 96 seconds; a longer
TTL means less traffic, but a stale address lingers that long when this host's
address changes.
At least 10 seconds and at most 24 hours.
.SS [Segment]
One or more are required.
.TP
.B Name=
Required.
A name that is unique within the file, used by
.BR mdnsctl (1)
to refer to the segment.
Quote it if it contains spaces.
.TP
.B Interfaces=
Required.
The interfaces of the segment, separated by commas and/or whitespace.
An interface need not exist when the daemon starts: it is waited for, and the
daemon follows it as it comes and goes.
.IP
A name may be a pattern:
.B *
stands for any run of characters, and
.B ?
for any one, so
.B enp*
is every interface whose name begins with
.BR enp .
Those that are there when the daemon starts, and those that come after, are
listened to; one that goes is forgotten, as one named is.
A name or pattern with a
.B !
before it is of interfaces the segment does not have, whatever else matches
them:
.B Interfaces=*,!br*
is every interface but the bridges.
Where it is written does not matter, a segment needs one that is not kept out,
and one that is named and kept out is an error.
What a segment says of its interfaces
.RB ( Untrusted= ,
and the other sections' names of them) is of those it has.
A loopback interface
.RB ( lo ,
or
.B lo0
on the BSDs) is not taken by a pattern, as what it hears is what this host
sends itself: name it to listen on it.
Two segments that share two or more interfaces are an error where the
interfaces are named alike, a mistake that is certain.
Where patterns merely overlap it is not: an interface may come that makes them
so, or never.
The segments then act as one that reflects what either would: a device is
reflected between two interfaces if any segment that has both says so, and what
is reflected of it is what any of them reflects of it together, whichever is
first in the file.
One segment cannot hold back what another reflects, whatever
.BR Untrusted= ,
.B ReflectedWhen=
and
.B ReflectedServices=
say: keep a segment that is to be narrower than another out of its interfaces
with
.BR ! .
.TP
.B Untrusted=
Optional.
Interfaces of the segment, in the same form as
.BR Interfaces= ,
that are announced nothing: the devices of an untrusted interface are still
announced to the other interfaces of the segment, but it is not told about
theirs.
This is how one network can be seen from another without seeing it back, for
example
.B Interfaces=br0,wifi
with
.B Untrusted=wifi
lets
.B br0
find the devices on
.B wifi
while
.B wifi
learns nothing about
.BR br0 .
Every one must be in
.BR Interfaces= ,
or be matched by a pattern in it, and at least one interface of a segment of
two or more must be trusted.
A pattern here is of the interfaces it matches: with
.B Interfaces=br0,iot*
it takes
.B Untrusted=iot*
to leave
.B br0
the one that is told.
Several untrusted interfaces do not learn about each other either.
Devices are still tracked on an untrusted interface, and listed by
.BR mdnsctl (1).
.TP
.B ForwardQuestions=
Optional.
Which questions asked on an interface of this segment the daemon asks on its
other interfaces, in a query of its own, so that the answer is reflected:
.B always
(the default) asks every one,
.B unknown
only those that nothing announced on the asking interface answers, and
.B never
none.
With
.BR unknown ,
a device that only answers when asked is not found once another device of its
type has been: a browse for that type is answered from the copy of the other.
Questions about a name a device on the asking interface owns, and those of a
device probing for its names, are never asked elsewhere; nor are those of an
untrusted interface.
.TP
.B NeighbourAddresses=
Optional:
.BR always ,
.B link\-local
(the default) or
.BR never .
Which of the addresses a device was given from the kernel's neighbour table
(see
.BR mdnsd (8))
to reflect with it, as well as those it announced.
.B link\-local
reflects those of a family the device announced a link-local address of, since
it speaks that family, if only on its own link: a device that announced only a
link-local IPv6 address is reflected with its routable IPv6 ones, and one that
announced only IPv4 is reflected with that alone.
.B always
reflects all of them, of any family, so that one too is reflected with its IPv6
addresses.
.B never
reflects only what devices announce: one whose only routable address of a
family came from the table is reflected without it, and one with no routable
address of its own not at all.
The addresses are learned all the same, and listed by
.BR mdnsctl (1).
.TP
.B ReflectedWhen=
Optional: a list of
.BR address ,
.B hostname
and
.BR service ,
separated by commas or spaces.
What a device that was heard on one interface of the segment must have, to be
reflected onto the others: a routable address, a hostname, and a service whose
host and port are known.
Default
.BR address,hostname,service .
Leaving out
.B service
reflects a device with none too, so that it is found by name (a lookup of
.IR host .local
from another network), though browsing finds nothing of it.
By default clients that only announce themselves, such as phones, are tracked
but not shown on the other networks; without
.B service
they are.
Services whose host and port are not known are never reflected.
Leaving out
.B address
reflects a device that has none a client could reach (only a link-local one, say)
by its services alone, with no address record: it is for the host to be found by
other means.
.B hostname
cannot be left out: a device is announced, and tracked, by its name.
An empty assignment resets it.
.TP
.B ReflectedServices=
Optional: service types, such as
.BR _ipp._tcp ,
separated by commas or spaces; the setting may be repeated.
Which of a device's services are reflected with it onto the other networks.
If any are named, only those are; a type with a
.B !
before it, as in
.BR !_ssh._tcp ,
is never reflected, and wins over naming it.
Default: every type.
The device itself is reflected with the services that are left, by its name and
addresses; when none is left it is not reflected at all.
It is an error to set it and leave
.B service
out of
.BR ReflectedWhen= :
every device would then be reflected, filtered out or not, by its name alone.
A type is written without
.BR .local ,
and without regard to case.
An empty assignment resets it.
.TP
.B Reannounce=
Optional boolean, default
.BR no .
Whether what is reflected onto the interfaces of this segment is announced
again at 80% of its TTL (see
.BR ReflectTTL= )
though it has not changed.
It need not be: a device that still wants a record asks for it again before
its TTL runs out, and is answered (RFC 6762 section 5.2).
What is new or has changed is announced twice, a second apart, either way.
An interface in several segments re-announces if any of them says so.
.TP
.B ReflectTTL=
Optional.
How long other networks keep the address of a device this segment reflects onto
them, and so, with
.BR Reannounce=yes ,
how often it is announced again: at 80% of it.
Default
.BR auto :
each device is announced with the TTLs it announced itself with (see
.BR "mdnsctl devices \-v" ).
Hosts conventionally say two minutes, so with re-announcing their address is
announced again every 96 seconds.
A time span, from 10 seconds to 24 hours, replaces the TTL of the
.I address
only: a longer one means fewer questions from the devices that want it, and,
re-announced, fewer announcements, but a stale address lingers that long in a
device that missed the goodbye when a device moves or vanishes.
The records of a device's services always keep their own TTL, and a segment
that re-announces does so before the shortest of them runs out.
A TTL below 10 seconds is raised to 10; a device that did not say gets two
minutes for its address and 75 minutes for its services.
The TTL is the one last heard, not counted down: a copy of a device that went
silent is withdrawn when the device expires.
An interface in several segments has what each reflects onto it announced with
that segment's TTL.
Independent of
.B TTL=
of
.BR [Advertise] .
.PP
A segment is a group of interfaces, as many as you like, whose devices are
reflected between
.I every pair
of them: read it as "these networks may see each other".
.IP \(bu 2
A segment with a single interface only tracks: its devices are listed by
.BR mdnsctl (1)
but nothing is reflected.
.IP \(bu 2
An interface may appear in several segments.
This is how one network can see two others that must not see each other.
If two segments would both announce a hostname on the interface they share, a
device of one interface that both reflect is announced as what they show of it
together, and, of devices of different interfaces, the one from the interface
with the lowest index wins, and the other takes over when it goes.
Reflection is never transitive, because a device reflected onto an interface is
not reflected again.
.IP \(bu 2
Two segments may not share two or more interfaces, since both would reflect
between that pair; sharing one interface is fine.
.IP \(bu 2
An interface may be listed only once per segment.
.SS [Interface]
Optional.
Settings of an interface, whichever segments it is in: devices are tracked per
interface, so what is kept of them is too.
There is at most one section for each name.
.TP
.B Name=
The interface, as it is written in
.B Interfaces=
of a segment, which must have it, or a pattern, for every interface it matches.
A section named for an interface is used before those named for a pattern; of
those, the first that sets a setting is the one, as in the order of the file.
Without a
.BR Name= ,
the section is for every interface: a setting it has applies to each interface
whose own section does not set it.
There is at most one such section.
.TP
.B IPv4=
What to listen for, and to announce, over IPv4 (the group 224.0.0.251): one of
.BR off ,
.B multicast
or
.BR on .
Default
.BR on .
.B off
does not use the family.
.B multicast
hears what is sent to the group, which is what devices send to be heard by
everyone on the link, and all that is asked of them.
.B on
hears that, and what is sent to the daemon's own address at the mDNS port,
5353, as well: the answers a device may give to one that asked, and from a
resolver that asks it directly.
Of what is sent there, only that from a host on a network the interface is on,
or link-local, is heard: a host that is routed to the address does not tell the
daemon of devices.
.TP
.B IPv6=
What to listen for, and to announce, over IPv6 (the group ff02::fb), as
.BR IPv4= .
.TP
.B MaxAddresses=
How many addresses of each family a device is kept with, and reflected with, at
most.
Default
.BR 8 :
enough for a global and a ULA IPv6 address, a few temporary ones and a
link-local one.
Addresses announced with the cache-flush bit replace those of their family, so
this only bounds what one device can pile up.
One announced beyond the limit takes the place of the one that runs out first, so a
device that moves on to a new temporary address is known by its newest.
At least 1; there is no upper bound, and
.B infinity
lifts the limit altogether, at the cost of letting one device announce as many
addresses as it likes.
.TP
.B MaxDevices=
How many devices the interface remembers at most; a host beyond that is not
learned of, until room is made by one that expires or says goodbye.
Default
.BR 1024 ,
which a sleep proxy or Thread border router speaking for several hosts does not
come near.
At least 1, or
.B infinity
for no limit, at the cost of letting a flood of invented devices grow the list
as far as memory allows.
A device takes about 1.6 KiB, and what the daemon does each time it wakes grows
with the number of devices it has, so a larger limit is for a network that
needs it.
.TP
.B MaxServices=
How many services a device is kept with, and reflected with, at most; more are
ignored.
Default
.BR 64 ,
far more than a device offers.
At least 1, or
.B infinity
for no limit, at the cost of letting one device announce as many as it likes.
.TP
.B MinimumLifetime=
Optional.
The least a device heard on this interface is kept after it was last heard from,
as a systemd time span such as
.BR 5min ,
.B 90s
or
.BR 1h ,
at most one day.
Default
.BR 1min .
A device lives for the longest TTL it announced, but never for less than this,
so that one that announces a TTL of a second, as a buggy one may, is not asked
about every few seconds.
.B 0
removes the minimum: each device then lives for exactly its longest TTL.
.IP
It is the price of a device that vanishes without saying goodbye: it stays
listed, and announced to the other networks, for up to this long.
A longer one keeps a device that goes quiet (one that sleeps on battery and
ignores questions, say) listed through the silence, so it is a setting for a
network of the devices that do, and a short one for a network of laptops and phones.
A device is asked to answer again a quarter of its life before the end where
that is less than
.BR RefreshBefore= ,
which counts back from the end of this lifetime.
.IP
It applies to the devices heard on the interface whichever segment reflects them.
.TP
.B RefreshBefore=
Ask a device heard on this interface to answer again this long before it would
expire, or one of its addresses would, as a time span like
.BR MinimumLifetime= .
Default
.BR 30s ;
.B 0
or
.B off
turns it off.
A device lives for as long as the longest TTL it announced, and at least
.B MinimumLifetime=
(one minute by default).
This long before that runs out, or when a quarter of its life is left if that is
less (see below), the daemon asks that device, and only it, for
the records it announced, and asks again about every quarter of this margin
until it answers or expires.
Where the family of its address is
.BR on ,
the first question is sent to the device's own address, not to the group, as
a device that sleeps may ignore the group and still answer what is sent to it;
the questions after it go to the group, and with
.B multicast
all do.
.IP
Each address of a device lives for the TTL it was announced with, at least the
minimum lifetime if there is one, so one the device stops announcing is dropped on its own, and
withdrawn from the other networks; a device is asked again this long before one
of its addresses would run out, too.
With
.B RefreshBefore=
off, a quiet device's addresses lapse after
.B MinimumLifetime=
even while its services live on.
A speaker whose records live seventy-five minutes is asked at about seventy-four
minutes.
Devices that are heard from regularly are never asked, and a device known only by
the address it was heard from has nothing to ask and is left alone.
It must be at least 30 seconds.
.IP
A device whose records live for less than four times this margin (two minutes,
with the default of 30 seconds, is the shortest that is asked the whole margin
before) is asked a quarter of its life before it ends instead, that is, three
quarters of the way through: one that lives for 20 seconds is asked at 15, and
is not asked the moment it is heard, nor over and over.
A device that does not answer is asked again about every quarter of the margin
(at least five seconds), until it expires.
.IP
Devices that go to sleep on battery may ignore questions, and what they
announced runs out as its TTL says: set
.B MinimumLifetime=
to keep them listed for longer, at the cost that a device that really vanishes
stays listed, and announced to the other networks, for up to that long.
.TP
.B Peer=
Optional.
Hosts this interface talks to alone, as addresses separated by commas or
spaces, and not to the mDNS groups: for a tunnel that cannot carry multicast,
such as WireGuard, with a daemon at the other end whose interface has this host
for its peer.
What is announced and asked on the interface is sent to each of them, and only
what they send to the daemon is heard, whatever it is sent to: they are who they
are by the tunnel they come through.
The interface is used over the families the peers are of, not by
.B IPv4=
and
.BR IPv6= ,
neither of which may be
.B off
for a family of one.
A device is not asked directly, at an address it has, on such an interface: its
peer is asked.
Repeated, it adds peers; empty, it resets.
It is for the section of one interface, not for every interface or a pattern.
Where the daemon cannot make sockets of an interface's own (the BSDs, and
Linux with
.BR portable-sockets )
it is an error.
Put the interface in a segment as any other: untrusted, a peer is told nothing
of the others and its devices are reflected to them; trusted, they are shown
each other's.
.TP
.B PeerPort=
Optional.
The port that peers are sent to, and that is listened at.
Default
.BR 5353 ,
the mDNS port: the peers hear this daemon from it, and the daemon at the other
end is set to the same.
.TP
.B ProbeInterval=
Also probe this interface for devices this often, as a time span: browse for
service types and instances.
Not set by default.
.B 0
or
.B off
turns it off for an interface that the section without a name sets it for.
It must be at least 10 seconds.
This is how a new quiet device that never announces itself is found; without it,
new devices are found at startup, when they announce themselves (most do when
they join a network) and by
.BR "mdnsctl probe" .
With
.B RefreshBefore=
off, a
.B ProbeInterval=
has to stay under the time devices are kept (their TTL, two minutes for most
addresses, or
.B MinimumLifetime=
if that is longer) to keep devices listed, and the daemon warns about it at
startup.
.TP
.B ProbeServices=
Optional.
Service types, such as
.BR _http._tcp ,
separated by commas or spaces, that every probe of this interface browses for
besides those its devices have announced.
Some devices answer only when asked for the type they offer, and never say it
in answer to a question for all types: a Shelly plug answers
.B _http._tcp
and nothing else.
Without the type being named here, such a device is found only when it
announces itself, which it does when it joins a network.
A probe runs at startup, whenever the interface comes up (also one that was not
there when the daemon started), on request and every
.BR ProbeInterval= ,
and each asks for these types.
None by default.
.PP
.B MinimumLifetime=
and
.B MaxAddresses=
used to be set in
.BR [Devices] ,
which is gone: either there is now an error that names the new place.
.PP
At least one of the two must not be
.BR off ,
for an interface that is named.
They choose the transports: each interface has a socket per family used, but
one list of devices for both, since mDNS records do not belong to the transport
they come over.
A device's addresses of both families are kept, whichever family it was heard
over, and announced over every family, so a client asking over IPv4 is told of
IPv6 addresses too.
.SH EXAMPLE
Three networks: a trusted LAN
.RB ( br0 ),
a media VLAN and an IoT Wi-Fi VLAN.
.B br0
and
.B media
see each other's devices, and so do
.B br0
and
.BR wifi ;
.B media
and
.B wifi
never see each other.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Ipc]
Socket=/run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock

[Advertise]
Hostname=router.local
TTL=2min

[Segment]
Name=media_stuff
Interfaces=br0,media

# One interface only: tracked, nothing reflected.
[Segment]
Name="IoT"
Interfaces=wifi

# br0 sees the devices on wifi, and wifi sees nothing of br0.
[Segment]
Name="IoT to br0"
Interfaces=br0,wifi
Untrusted=wifi

# For every interface.
[Interface]
IPv4=on
IPv6=on
MaxAddresses=8
MaxDevices=1024
MaxServices=64
RefreshBefore=30s

# Some IoT devices sleep and ignore multicast: keep them listed for an hour.
[Interface]
Name=wifi
MinimumLifetime=1h
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
Default location.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdnsd (8),
.BR mdnsctl (1)
